## DESCRIPTION
## Quantitative Reasoning: Doubling time - size of a population after a given time, with a written justification
## Writing practice for the communication (MF2) part of the in-person final:
## decide whether the population passes a threshold within a given time and justify it.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Algebra')
## DBchapter('Exponential and logarithmic expressions and functions')
## DBsection('Applications and models - population growth')
## Date('11/18/26')
## Author('Erich Jauch')
## Institution('Westminster College (Missouri)')
## KEYWORDS('exponential growth','doubling time','writing')


DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",    # Standard macros for PG language
    "PGML.pl",          # PGML markup and Math Objects
    "parserPopUp.pl",
    "PGessaymacros.pl",
    "decimalPlaces.pl",
    "PGcourse.pl",      # Customization file for the course
);

# Part (a) is the same as DoublingTime2.pg.
# Part (b) matches the in-person final's Q4 add-on: would the population pass a
# threshold within a different time? The yes/no and the supporting value are graded
# automatically; the justification is graded by hand (0-4 scoring guide).

$pop = random(9,15,1)*1000;

$time = random(12,34,2);

$check = random($time,4*$time,1);

@ans = ();

$ans[0] = $pop*2**($check/$time);


# HINTS (added from FA24 WeBWorK answers): messages for common wrong answers.
$roundMsg = "It looks like you rounded the exponent (the number of doubling times). Keep all of its "
          . "digits, or enter the whole calculation at once, and round only the final answer.";
$wholeMsg = "It looks like you counted only whole doubling times. The number of doubling times "
          . "($check divided by $time) does not have to be a whole number; use it exactly as the exponent.";
$closeMsg = "Your answer is close, but not exact. Keep all of the digits of the exponent and round "
          . "only the final answer.";
$e = $check/$time;
$botHints = [
    [ $pop*2**Round($e, 2), $roundMsg ],
    [ $pop*2**Round($e, 1), $roundMsg ],
    [ $pop*2**int($e),      $wholeMsg ],
];

# Part (b): a whole number of doubling times, different from the time in part (a),
# and a round-number threshold about 15% above or below the population then.
do { $k = random(2,4,1) } until ($k*$time != $check);
$check2 = $k*$time;
$later  = $pop*2**$k;
$above  = random(0,1,1);
$thresh = 1000*Round($later*($above ? 0.85 : 1.15)/1000, 0);
$passes = $later > $thresh ? 'Yes' : 'No';
$passPopUp = PopUp([ 'Choose', 'Yes', 'No' ], $passes);

$laterHints = [
    [ $pop*2*$k, "The population doubles every $time minutes, so after $k doubling times you "
               . "multiply by 2 a total of $k times, not by 2 times $k." ],
];

$compare   = $passes eq 'Yes' ? 'more' : 'fewer';
$aRounded = Round($ans[0], 0);    # for the solution

BEGIN_PGML
In a sci-fi film the evil nanobots double in numbers every [$time] minutes. Suppose there are
[$pop] nanobots to start with.

a) There would be [_]{DecimalPlacesCmp($ans[0], 0, hints => $botHints, nearMiss => 0.01, nearMissMessage => $closeMsg)}
nanobots after [$check] minutes (rounded to the nearest whole number).

b) Would there be more than [$thresh] nanobots after [$check2] minutes? [_]{$passPopUp}

    How many nanobots would there be after [$check2] minutes?
    [_]{DecimalPlacesCmp($later, 0, hints => $laterHints)} nanobots

    Justify your answer in one or two sentences using your work. A complete
    justification states the conclusion and supports it with numbers from this problem.
    Your justification will be graded by your instructor.

[@ ANS(essay_cmp()); essay_box(5, 60) @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) [$check] minutes is [`[$check]/[$time]`] doubling times, so there are
[`[$pop] \times 2^{[$check]/[$time]} \approx [$aRounded]`] nanobots (rounded).

b) [$passes]. A model justification:

>> "[$check2] minutes is [$check2]/[$time] = [$k] doubling times, so there would be
[$pop] [`\times 2^{[$k]}`] = [$later] nanobots. That is [$compare] than [$thresh], so the answer is
[$passes]." <<
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
